window.FAA_INDEX = [{"k":"module","m":"01","t":"01 · The Fund Industry & Structures","h":"The Fund Industry & Structures","u":"modules/01-industry-and-structures.html","x":"The Fund Industry & Structures Before you can account for a fund you need to know who else touches its money and data. Most \"fund accounting problems\" are really handoff problems between these parties."},{"k":"module","m":"01","t":"01 · The Fund Industry & Structures","h":"The cast of characters","u":"modules/01-industry-and-structures.html#the-cast-of-characters","x":"Party What they do Why you care Investor (LP / shareholder) Puts money in and owns a share of the fund. Their capital account or share balance is what you ultimately report. General Partner (GP) / Board Legally controls the fund and appoints service providers. In a hedge fund the GP is often an affiliate of the manager. Signs off financials and policy decisions (e.g. valuation policy). Investment manager (IM) Makes the investment decisions; earns management and performance fees. Source of trades, pricing views, and most urgent questions. Fund administrator Independent party that keeps the books, strikes the NAV, processes investor dealing, produces statements. This is usually you. Independence from the manager is the whole value proposition. Custodian Safekeeps assets (typically for long-only / regulated funds). Holds title. Source of position and cash statements to reconcile to. Prime broker (PB) Executes/clears, finances leverage, lends stock for shorts, holds collateral for hedge funds. Daily statements; margin, financing and borrow charges. Auditor Gives an opinion on annual financial statements. Your records must support every number; expect PBC (provided-by-client) lists. Transfer agent Maintains the shareholder register (common for mutual funds and ETFs). Share counts must agree to the fund's books. Legal counsel / tax advisor Offering documents, side letters, tax reporting (K-1, PFIC, etc.). Define the rules your system has to implement."},{"k":"module","m":"01","t":"01 · The Fund Industry & Structures","h":"Fund types and what changes in the accounting","u":"modules/01-industry-and-structures.html#fund-types-and-what-changes-in-the-accounting","x":"Type Typical features Accounting emphasis Hedge fund Long/short, derivatives, leverage; monthly (sometimes weekly/daily) dealing; partnership or corporation. Daily P&L, derivatives, financing, investor-level fees (HWM, equalization, series). Mutual fund / UCITS / ETF Open-ended, daily NAV, regulated, strict diversification rules. Daily NAV accuracy, shares and NAV per share, distributions, regulatory ratios. Private equity / venture Capital calls and distributions over 8–12 years; illiquid holdings. Commitments, waterfalls, fair-value marks, IRR/TVPI. Real estate / credit funds Loans or property; income-driven. Interest accruals, amortization, impairments, loan servicing data. Fund of funds Invests in other funds. Valuation off other funds' estimated NAVs; lag between estimate and final NAV."},{"k":"module","m":"01","t":"01 · The Fund Industry & Structures","h":"Common structures","u":"modules/01-industry-and-structures.html#common-structures","x":"Master–feeder Onshore (US taxable) and offshore (non-US / tax-exempt) investors each invest in their own feeder ; both feeders invest in a single master that holds the portfolio. The master's P&L is allocated to feeders by their share of master capital; each feeder then allocates to its own investors and charges its own fees. Feeder A US LP Feeder B Cayman Ltd Master fund Holds all positions & trades Prime brokers Custody & financing Series and classes One legal fund can offer different classes (different fee terms, currencies, or hedging) and, in some hedge funds, monthly series of shares so each subscription tracks its own performance-fee base. We cover this properly in Module 06. Side pocket / SPV Illiquid or hard-to-value investments can be segregated so redeeming investors don't take a mark they can't realise. Only investors who held at designation participate. Dealing frequency drives workload. A daily-NAV mutual fund can't rely on month-end clean-up; a monthly hedge fund can estimate daily but must be exactly right on the dealing day. Always ask: \"When is the NAV used to transact?\""},{"k":"module","m":"01","t":"01 · The Fund Industry & Structures","h":"What drives the cost of running a fund","u":"modules/01-industry-and-structures.html#what-drives-the-cost-of-running-a-fund","x":"Funds are priced on asset-based fees to the administrator (basis points on NAV) and often minimums per share class or investor. Complex structures (many series, side pockets, FX-hedged classes) cost more to run — useful context when someone asks \"why does this take so long?\". Practice Pick a fund you work on and draw its structure: investors → feeder(s) → master → brokers/custodian. Write beside each box which system holds the data. You'll use that map to chase breaks."},{"k":"module","m":"02","t":"02 · Accounting Fundamentals for Funds","h":"Accounting Fundamentals for Funds","u":"modules/02-accounting-fundamentals.html","x":"Accounting Fundamentals for Funds A fund is just a set of books where the \"business\" is holding investments. Everything in fund accounting reduces to double entry, applied to securities and investors."},{"k":"module","m":"02","t":"02 · Accounting Fundamentals for Funds","h":"The equation and the statements","u":"modules/02-accounting-fundamentals.html#the-equation-and-the-statements","x":"Assets = Liabilities + Net assets (equity). In a fund, net assets belong to investors; that figure is the NAV . Net assets are broken down into each investor's capital account (partnership) or shares × NAV per share (corporation/mutual fund). Type Examples in a fund Normal balance Assets Investments at fair value, cash, receivable for investments sold, interest and dividends receivable, unrealized gain on derivatives, margin deposits Debit Liabilities Payable for investments purchased, accrued fees and expenses, redemptions payable, securities sold short, unrealized loss on derivatives Credit Equity Investor capital / shares Credit Income Dividends, interest, realized and unrealized gains Credit Expenses Management fee, performance fee, admin, audit, legal, interest expense, borrow fees Debit"},{"k":"module","m":"02","t":"02 · Accounting Fundamentals for Funds","h":"Double entry in 60 seconds","u":"modules/02-accounting-fundamentals.html#double-entry-in-60-seconds","x":"Every transaction has equal debits and credits . Debits increase assets and expenses; credits increase liabilities, equity and income. If the entry doesn't balance, something is missing — which is itself a useful control (a system that cannot post an unbalanced entry). Worked example: buy 1,000 shares at $50, commission $25 Account Debit Credit Investments at cost 50,025 Payable for investments purchased (until settlement) 50,025 Commissions on purchases normally become part of cost; on sales they reduce proceeds. Settlement day: debit the payable, credit cash."},{"k":"module","m":"02","t":"02 · Accounting Fundamentals for Funds","h":"Trade date vs settlement date","u":"modules/02-accounting-fundamentals.html#trade-date-vs-settlement-date","x":"Investment companies book on trade date : the day the fund becomes economically exposed. The cash moves later, on settlement date (T+1 for most US equities since May 2024; varies by market). Between the two there's a payable or receivable. Get this wrong and your cash looks fine while your NAV is wrong. Trade date (T) Book position & P&L exposure; set up payable/receivable Between Position is priced; cash unchanged Settle date Cash moves; payable/receivable cleared Try it: a fund buys 500 shares at $20 on Monday and settles on Wednesday. What is on its books on Tuesday? The position is already there: Investments 10,000 (plus any commission) and a payable for investments purchased 10,000 . Cash is untouched and NAV is unchanged, because the trade is booked on trade date."},{"k":"module","m":"02","t":"02 · Accounting Fundamentals for Funds","h":"Accrual accounting","u":"modules/02-accounting-fundamentals.html#accrual-accounting","x":"Income and expenses are recognised when earned or incurred, not when cash moves. For funds this means daily accruals of bond interest, dividend income from the ex-date (not pay date), management fees, admin fees and interest on financing. Ex-date, record date, pay date. Dividends are recognised on the ex-dividend date. The fund records a dividend receivable; when cash arrives on the pay date the receivable clears. Short positions owe the dividend (\"dividend on short\") on the same ex-date."},{"k":"module","m":"02","t":"02 · Accounting Fundamentals for Funds","h":"Realized vs unrealized P&L","u":"modules/02-accounting-fundamentals.html#realized-vs-unrealized-p-l","x":"Unrealized : change in value of positions still held. Moves every time the price or FX rate moves. Realized : locked in at sale or cover. Realized = proceeds − cost (of the lots relieved). When a position is sold, the previously recorded unrealized gain is reversed and realized gain recorded, so total P&L doesn't double count. Event Entry Buy at 100, price rises to 108 Dr Investments (unrealized appreciation) 8 / Cr Net change in unrealized gain (income) 8 - carrying value moves from cost 100 to fair value 108 Sell at 108 Dr Cash (or receivable) 108 / Cr Investments at cost 100 / Cr Realized gain 8; then reverse the 8 of unrealized (Dr Net change in unrealized 8 / Cr Investments unrealized appreciation 8) Total period P&L is the same either way; what changes is classification — and that matters for tax and for reporting realized vs unrealized."},{"k":"module","m":"02","t":"02 · Accounting Fundamentals for Funds","h":"Cost basis and lot relief","u":"modules/02-accounting-fundamentals.html#cost-basis-and-lot-relief","x":"When you sell part of a position bought in several lots you must choose which lots are relieved. Methods: FIFO , LIFO , HIFO (highest cost first), average cost , or specific lot . The method set in the fund's policy drives realized P&L and tax character. Always confirm the method before debugging a \"wrong\" gain."},{"k":"module","m":"02","t":"02 · Accounting Fundamentals for Funds","h":"Foreign currency","u":"modules/02-accounting-fundamentals.html#foreign-currency","x":"A fund has a base (functional) currency . Positions are held in local currency and translated at the daily FX rate. For investment funds, FX effects on holdings are often reported as part of the net realized/unrealized gain on investments; separating \"market\" from \"currency\" gain is a reporting choice (ASC 946 allows combining)."},{"k":"module","m":"02","t":"02 · Accounting Fundamentals for Funds","h":"Try it","u":"modules/02-accounting-fundamentals.html#try-it","x":"The journal entry trainer gives you buy, sell, accrual and fee scenarios and checks your debits and credits. Settlement cycle note T+1 applies to most US/Canada securities since 28 May 2024; many other markets (and some instruments like FX or bonds) still differ. Check each market's convention, don't assume."},{"k":"module","m":"03","t":"03 · The NAV Cycle","h":"The NAV Cycle","u":"modules/03-nav-cycle.html","x":"The NAV Cycle Net asset value is the product of the whole department. A senior fund accountant can explain every line of it and every movement versus yesterday."},{"k":"module","m":"03","t":"03 · The NAV Cycle","h":"The formula","u":"modules/03-nav-cycle.html#the-formula","x":"NAV = Total assets (at fair value) − Total liabilities. Then NAV per share = NAV ÷ shares outstanding for share-based funds; for partnerships, NAV is allocated to capital accounts instead. Gross vs net. \"GAV\" (gross asset value) ignores some liabilities, and many managers talk about \"NAV before fees\". Always confirm which definition a number uses, and whether performance fee accrual is in it."},{"k":"module","m":"03","t":"03 · The NAV Cycle","h":"The daily process","u":"modules/03-nav-cycle.html#the-daily-process","x":"1 Capture Trades from manager / OMS; corporate actions; cash movements 2 Price Securities, FX, curves from approved sources 3 Accrue Interest, dividends, fees, expenses, financing 4 Reconcile Positions and cash vs brokers/custodian 5 Review Reasonableness checks on the draft NAV 6 Release Sign-off, distribute, archive support Capture : trades booked on trade date with correct quantity, price, fees, settle date, broker, strategy/fund allocation. Unmatched or late trades are the #1 cause of NAV errors. Price : apply the pricing policy (see Module 05). Check for stale and missing prices before they hit the NAV. Accrue : bond coupons, swap legs, borrow costs, management fee, admin fee, audit accrual. Reconcile : at least cash and positions daily where possible; the rest by month end (Module 08). Review : the checks below. Release : documented sign-off, then NAV goes to the manager (and investors/transfer agent). Try it: NAV per share fell 3% on a day the market rose 1%. Name three things to check first. 1) Price and FX on the largest positions (stale price, wrong quote unit, wrong rate). 2) Trades and corporate actions : a duplicate trade, or a missed split or merger. 3) Accruals and liabilities : a large fee, a missing accrual reversal, or a redemption payable booked twice. Then compare P&L by position against the market."},{"k":"module","m":"03","t":"03 · The NAV Cycle","h":"Month-end additions","u":"modules/03-nav-cycle.html#month-end-additions","x":"Final prices and FX; investor dealing: subscriptions and redemptions struck at the dealing-day NAV. Management and performance fees crystallised or accrued per terms; allocation of P&L to investors. Full position / cash / third-party reconciliations; clearing old breaks. Expense true-ups (audit, legal, organizational costs amortization). Investor statements, financial reporting packages, trial balance review."},{"k":"module","m":"03","t":"03 · The NAV Cycle","h":"Reasonableness checks on a draft NAV","u":"modules/03-nav-cycle.html#reasonableness-checks-on-a-draft-nav","x":"Check What it catches NAV change vs expected return (manager estimate or benchmark) Missing trade, bad price, wrong FX Top positions: price move vs market Stale or mistyped price; wrong currency P&L by position, largest contributors and detractors Outliers that explain the move Cash by currency vs bank / broker, negative balances Unbooked settlement, FX trade missing Accruals vs prior day, interest vs rate × balance Missing or doubled accrual Capital activity: shares, subs, reds vs transfer agent Dealing errors Flat-price check: prices unchanged for N days Stale pricing Exposure vs mandate (concentration, leverage) Booking errors; also compliance flags to escalate"},{"k":"module","m":"03","t":"03 · The NAV Cycle","h":"When the NAV is wrong","u":"modules/03-nav-cycle.html#when-the-nav-is-wrong","x":"An error discovered after release is a NAV error . Typical steps: quantify the impact by day, check against the fund's materiality threshold (often 0.5% of NAV for equity funds, lower for money-market style funds; it is set by policy, not by you), notify the manager, determine who is compensated (fund vs investors who dealt at the wrong price), correct, and document root cause and prevention. Never silently fix. Corrections must be visible and approved. A hidden adjustment, even a small one, is a control failure."},{"k":"module","m":"03","t":"03 · The NAV Cycle","h":"Estimate vs final NAV","u":"modules/03-nav-cycle.html#estimate-vs-final-nav","x":"Hedge funds often publish an estimate a few days after month end and a final weeks later after statements arrive. Understand the tolerance for differences, and which investors transacted at which."},{"k":"module","m":"04","t":"04 · Instruments & How to Book Them","h":"Instruments & How to Book Them","u":"modules/04-instruments.html","x":"Instruments & How to Book Them For each instrument ask four questions: What is the position? What is the cash? What accrues daily? What does the broker statement show?"},{"k":"module","m":"04","t":"04 · Instruments & How to Book Them","h":"Equities (long and short)","u":"modules/04-instruments.html#equities-long-and-short","x":"Long : buy at cost, carry at fair value, dividends from ex-date. Short : borrow and sell. The position is a liability (securities sold short) at fair value. Proceeds are held by the PB. You owe dividends and pay a borrow fee on the market value. Gains come from falling prices. Commission and fees affect cost (buys) or proceeds (sells)."},{"k":"module","m":"04","t":"04 · Instruments & How to Book Them","h":"Bonds and accrued interest","u":"modules/04-instruments.html#bonds-and-accrued-interest","x":"Bonds are quoted clean (without accrued interest). You pay dirty = clean + accrued . The accrued amount you pay a seller is not an expense; it's recorded as interest receivable purchased and netted against the first coupon. Accrued interest = Par × coupon rate × (days accrued ÷ day-count basis). Convention Days Typical use 30/360 30-day months, 360-day year US corporates, agencies, munis ACT/ACT Actual days ÷ actual days in period US Treasuries, many govvies ACT/360 Actual days ÷ 360 Money market, loans, floating rate notes ACT/365 Actual days ÷ 365 UK gilts (historic), some loans/swaps Also: premium/discount amortization (straight-line or effective yield per policy), OID , inflation-linked principal adjustments, and paydowns on amortizing securities (reduce face and cost proportionally). Try the accrued interest calculator . Try it: par $100,000, 6% coupon, 30/360, last coupon 15 Jan, settlement 15 Mar. What is the accrued interest? 30/360 gives 60 days (2 months x 30). Accrued = 100,000 x 6% x 60 / 360 = $1,000.00 . Check it in the slider above by setting the dates."},{"k":"module","m":"04","t":"04 · Instruments & How to Book Them","h":"FX spot and forwards","u":"modules/04-instruments.html#fx-spot-and-forwards","x":"Spot : settles in T+2 for most pairs. Book on trade date. Forward : value = (agreed forward rate − current forward rate for the remaining term) × notional, discounted. The unrealized P&L appears as an asset or liability. At maturity it becomes realized when the currencies exchange. Hedged share classes : the forward's P&L is allocated only to the hedged class."},{"k":"module","m":"04","t":"04 · Instruments & How to Book Them","h":"Futures","u":"modules/04-instruments.html#futures","x":"Futures carry no purchase price; you post initial margin (an asset held with the broker, not a cost) and receive/pay variation margin daily. Daily variation margin settles P&L in cash, so the future's fair value after settlement is effectively zero and the gain flows through realized/unrealized P&L. Remember contract multiplier (e.g. S&P e-mini = $50 × index)."},{"k":"module","m":"04","t":"04 · Instruments & How to Book Them","h":"Options","u":"modules/04-instruments.html#options","x":"Buying : premium paid is cost; mark-to-market via market price or model. Expire worthless → realized loss of premium. Writing : premium received creates a liability at fair value; closing or expiry realizes the difference. Exercise/assignment : option cost/proceeds rolls into the cost of the underlying acquired or the proceeds of the underlying sold. Check: multiplier (100 for equity options), expiry dates (and after-hours exercise), early assignment risk on short in-the-money calls around ex-dividend."},{"k":"module","m":"04","t":"04 · Instruments & How to Book Them","h":"Swaps","u":"modules/04-instruments.html#swaps","x":"Swap Cash flows Valuation Interest-rate swap Fixed vs floating on notional; net settlement per period PV of fixed leg − PV of floating leg; accrue both legs daily Total-return swap (TRS) Return on a reference asset vs financing (e.g. SOFR + spread) Reference asset P&L + financing accrual; resets monthly/quarterly Credit default swap (CDS) Fixed premium for protection; payment on credit event Upfront payment + MTM from spread curve; accrue premium Common pitfalls: reset dates, notional changes after partial unwinds, mismatch between the dealer's valuation and the fund's model."},{"k":"module","m":"04","t":"04 · Instruments & How to Book Them","h":"Repos, loans, and others","u":"modules/04-instruments.html#repos-loans-and-others","x":"Repo is a collateralized loan: the cash lent/borrowed accrues interest, the collateral isn't derecognised. Bank loans have floating rates with resets, PIK interest and agency fee notices. Treat each as \"cash + schedule + accrual\" and tie out to the agent's notices."},{"k":"module","m":"04","t":"04 · Instruments & How to Book Them","h":"Corporate actions","u":"modules/04-instruments.html#corporate-actions","x":"Event Effect on books Cash dividend Receivable from ex-date; tax withholding creates a reclaimable asset if applicable Stock split / reverse split Quantity changes, cost unchanged; price per share changes inversely Stock dividend / spin-off New position; allocate cost between old and new by relative market value (per tax guidance) Merger (cash/stock) Old position closed, cash and/or new shares received; realized gain and new cost basis Rights/warrants Zero or allocated cost; exercise deadlines matter Name/ticker/identifier change No P&L; keep the history linked (identifier mapping) Habit Maintain a corporate-action calendar for each fund's holdings. Most nasty NAV errors are unprocessed corporate actions discovered when a position break appears."},{"k":"module","m":"05","t":"05 · Pricing & Fair Value","h":"Pricing & Fair Value","u":"modules/05-pricing-fair-value.html","x":"Pricing & Fair Value Price is the largest single driver of NAV. A senior accountant doesn't just load prices, they can defend them."},{"k":"module","m":"05","t":"05 · Pricing & Fair Value","h":"Where prices come from","u":"modules/05-pricing-fair-value.html#where-prices-come-from","x":"Vendors (Bloomberg, ICE, Refinitiv, Markit, etc.): exchange closes and evaluated prices for OTC instruments. Brokers / dealers : quotes for illiquid bonds, loans, derivatives. Manager marks : for hard-to-value assets, subject to the fund's valuation policy and administrator challenge. Models : DCF, comparables, option models. Inputs must be observable where possible. The valuation policy defines primary and backup sources, cut-off time (e.g. 4 p.m. NY), which side of the market to use (bid for longs, ask for shorts, or mid), and who can override."},{"k":"module","m":"05","t":"05 · Pricing & Fair Value","h":"The fair value hierarchy (ASC 820 / IFRS 13)","u":"modules/05-pricing-fair-value.html#the-fair-value-hierarchy-asc-820-ifrs-13","x":"Level Inputs Examples Level 1 Quoted prices in active markets for identical assets Listed equities, open-ended mutual funds with daily NAV, exchange-traded futures Level 2 Observable inputs other than Level 1 quotes Corporate bonds priced by evaluated vendor, OTC FX forwards, swaps with observable curves Level 3 Unobservable inputs; significant judgement Private equity stakes, distressed loans, illiquid structured credit The level is determined by the lowest significant input. Level 3 positions need a rollforward and disclosure of unobservable inputs in the financial statements. NAV-as-practical-expedient. Investments in other funds can be valued at the investee's reported NAV per share and disclosed separately from the hierarchy when the practical expedient is used (ASC 820-10-35-59)."},{"k":"module","m":"05","t":"05 · Pricing & Fair Value","h":"Price challenge process","u":"modules/05-pricing-fair-value.html#price-challenge-process","x":"Exception report: no price, stale price (unchanged for N days), large move vs prior day, move vs peers. Compare against a second source. Investigate corporate actions, currency, quote basis (clean/dirty, % of par vs absolute). Escalate to the manager with evidence, not opinion: \"Vendor A 98.5, Vendor B 98.4, your mark 101.0 — please support.\" Where the manager insists, document the override, approver, rationale, and take it to the pricing/valuation committee if the policy requires."},{"k":"module","m":"05","t":"05 · Pricing & Fair Value","h":"Frequent pricing traps","u":"modules/05-pricing-fair-value.html#frequent-pricing-traps","x":"Quote units : UK equities in pence (GBp) vs pounds; bonds quoted as % of par; futures with tick values. Stale prices on holidays; thinly traded names; delisted or suspended securities (price may be zero or last). FX timing : FX rate cut (e.g. WM/Reuters 4pm London) must match policy. Bid/ask vs last : consistent application matters more than which is \"right\". Same security, two identifiers (CUSIP/ISIN/SEDOL/FIGI) → duplicate or missing prices."},{"k":"module","m":"05","t":"05 · Pricing & Fair Value","h":"Pricing committee & independence","u":"modules/05-pricing-fair-value.html#pricing-committee-independence","x":"The administrator's value is independent verification. If the manager both provides and approves marks for Level 3 assets, make sure the administrator receives evidence (valuation reports, third-party appraisals) and records the review. For difficult cases, auditors will ask for this trail."},{"k":"module","m":"06","t":"06 · Investor Accounting","h":"Investor Accounting","u":"modules/06-investor-accounting.html","x":"Investor Accounting The portfolio side tells you what the fund is worth. The investor side tells you who owns what. This is where most senior-level complexity lives."},{"k":"module","m":"06","t":"06 · Investor Accounting","h":"Two models","u":"modules/06-investor-accounting.html#two-models","x":"Share-based (corporation / mutual fund) Capital-account-based (partnership / many hedge funds) Ownership Shares × NAV per share Each investor has a capital account in dollars Dealing Issue/redeem shares at NAV per share Add/withdraw capital at NAV; allocate P&L to accounts Performance fees Often series accounting or equalization to keep per-share fairness Computed per investor (crystallised on their own HWM)"},{"k":"module","m":"06","t":"06 · Investor Accounting","h":"Capital account rollforward","u":"modules/06-investor-accounting.html#capital-account-rollforward","x":"Line Amount Opening capital 1,000,000 + Subscriptions 200,000 − Redemptions (100,000) + Allocated trading P&L 60,000 − Management fee (2,000) − Performance fee (9,600) − Allocated expenses (1,400) Closing capital 1,147,000 The sum of all investors' closing capital must equal fund NAV. That tie-out is the single most important check on the investor side. Try it: fund NAV is 5,000,000 but the investors' closing balances add up to 4,987,500. Where do you look? A 12,500 gap . Check, in order: a late subscription or redemption not in the capital accounts; a fee or expense accrued in the portfolio books but not allocated; an allocation run on stale data before a late trade was fixed; then rounding across many investors."},{"k":"module","m":"06","t":"06 · Investor Accounting","h":"Allocating P&L","u":"modules/06-investor-accounting.html#allocating-p-l","x":"Common approaches: Pro-rata by beginning capital (with a capital-weighted adjustment when money moves mid-period). Daily/valuation-date allocation : P&L each dealing date goes to capital at that time. Cleanest when flows occur intra-month. Special allocations : expenses charged only to a class; hedged-class FX P&L; side-pocket P&L to participating investors only. Use the allocation calculator to see how weighting changes who gets what."},{"k":"module","m":"06","t":"06 · Investor Accounting","h":"Subscriptions and redemptions","u":"modules/06-investor-accounting.html#subscriptions-and-redemptions","x":"Dealing day : the NAV on which units are struck. Subscription money is typically received before (cash in a \"subscriptions received in advance\" liability) and units issued at the dealing NAV. Notice periods and lock-ups : redemption requests (e.g. 45 days' notice, quarterly) are recorded as redemptions payable liabilities from the dealing day, reducing NAV even though cash leaves later. Gates : the fund limits total redemptions in a period (e.g. 20% of NAV); excess is carried forward pro-rata. Holdbacks : e.g. 5–10% of a full redemption withheld until audit."},{"k":"module","m":"06","t":"06 · Investor Accounting","h":"Why equalization or series exist","u":"modules/06-investor-accounting.html#why-equalization-or-series-exist","x":"If a fund charges a performance fee on fund performance, an investor joining after a good month would either pay a fee on gains made before they joined, or the older investors would be undercharged. Two fixes: Series accounting Each subscription month is a separate series with its own NAV and HWM. Gains are tracked per series; periodically (e.g. annually) series with positive performance roll up to a lead series. Fair, but more share classes to manage. Equalization (equalisation factor / credit) Everyone is in one class. A late entrant who subscribes at a NAV already net of an accrued performance fee pays an equalization credit (or buys extra shares) to compensate; if the NAV is below the HWM they pay a depreciation deposit . The goal is that every investor's performance fee equals what they'd pay for their own return. Key idea. The fund can only have one NAV per share per class. Everything else (series, equalization) is machinery that lets each investor still pay a fee that reflects their own gain."},{"k":"module","m":"06","t":"06 · Investor Accounting","h":"Side pockets and designated investments","u":"modules/06-investor-accounting.html#side-pockets-and-designated-investments","x":"An illiquid position is moved to a separate class; investors at that time receive a proportional special share class. New subscribers don't share. Redeemers get their main-class balance but keep the side pocket until realization. Fees often follow different rules: usually charged only on realised gains."},{"k":"module","m":"06","t":"06 · Investor Accounting","h":"Transfers, tax and withholding","u":"modules/06-investor-accounting.html#transfers-tax-and-withholding","x":"Transfers between investors need legal approval and carry cost basis and HWM. US partnerships issue K-1s (tax allocations differ from book allocations — \"book/tax differences\"). Withholding on non-US investors is handled separately from NAV accounting."},{"k":"module","m":"06","t":"06 · Investor Accounting","h":"Controls specific to investor accounting","u":"modules/06-investor-accounting.html#controls-specific-to-investor-accounting","x":"Capital accounts sum to NAV every dealing date. Transfer-agent register ties to share counts. Subscriptions agree to bank statements (don't issue units for unreceived cash without approval). Redemptions checked to documents, notice dates, lock-ups, gates, KYC/AML clearance."},{"k":"module","m":"07","t":"07 · Fees, Expenses & Waterfalls","h":"Fees, Expenses & Waterfalls","u":"modules/07-fees-and-waterfalls.html","x":"Fees, Expenses & Waterfalls Fees are contractual math applied to NAV. Mistakes here create investor complaints, so read the offering document, not just the system configuration."},{"k":"module","m":"07","t":"07 · Fees, Expenses & Waterfalls","h":"Management fee","u":"modules/07-fees-and-waterfalls.html#management-fee","x":"Fee = NAV × annual rate × days ÷ basis (365 or 360; or rate ÷ 12 per month). Things the documents specify: Which NAV: opening , closing , average , before or after accruing performance fee , before or after redemptions. Paid monthly or quarterly, in advance or arrears. Treatment of side pockets (often a lower rate). Fee on committed capital vs invested capital (private funds)."},{"k":"module","m":"07","t":"07 · Fees, Expenses & Waterfalls","h":"Performance (incentive) fee","u":"modules/07-fees-and-waterfalls.html#performance-incentive-fee","x":"Charged on profit above specified hurdles. The key terms: Term Meaning Fee rate Typically 10–30% of profits above thresholds (20% is classic). High-water mark (HWM) No fee until the investor's NAV exceeds its highest prior end-of-period value (after fees). Losses must be recovered first. Hurdle rate Minimum return before fee. Soft hurdle : once cleared, fee on all profit above HWM. Hard hurdle : fee only on the excess over the hurdle. Crystallization When accrued fee becomes payable (annually, quarterly, or on redemption). Loss carry-forward Alternative to HWM at investor level for shared accounts. Clawback Return of previously paid incentive if later losses exceed (typical in PE). Worked example (soft vs hard hurdle) Opening NAV 100, HWM 100, closing before fee 110, hurdle 5%, rate 20%. No hurdle: chargeable = 10 → fee 2.00 . Soft 5% hurdle: hurdle level 105 is cleared; chargeable = 110 − 100 = 10 → fee 2.00 . Hard 5% hurdle: chargeable = 110 − 105 = 5 → fee 1.00 . HWM 120: 110 < 120 → fee 0 ; the new HWM stays 120. Try your own numbers in the performance fee calculator . Redemptions mid-period. A redeemer crystallizes the accrued fee on the redeemed part. Many errors come from forgetting that the fee on redeemed capital is paid out on that date. Try it: opening NAV 200, HWM 220, NAV before fee 230, 20% fee, no hurdle. What is the fee? The fund only clears the HWM by 10 (230 - 220), so the fee is 20% x 10 = 2.0 , not 20% of the 30 gain. New NAV is 228 and the HWM resets to 228."},{"k":"module","m":"07","t":"07 · Fees, Expenses & Waterfalls","h":"Fund expenses","u":"modules/07-fees-and-waterfalls.html#fund-expenses","x":"Operating : admin, audit, legal, directors, custody, registered office, regulatory fees. Trading-related : commissions, financing, borrow, interest. Organizational costs : expensed (US GAAP) or amortised (some jurisdictions/IFRS variations); offering costs are typically deferred and amortised for a defined period. Expense caps / waivers : manager reimburses above a cap; recorded as a receivable or expense reduction. Accrue to a budget daily and true-up when invoices arrive; large true-ups are a signal that the accrual basis was wrong."},{"k":"module","m":"07","t":"07 · Fees, Expenses & Waterfalls","h":"Private-fund distribution waterfalls","u":"modules/07-fees-and-waterfalls.html#private-fund-distribution-waterfalls","x":"Proceeds are applied in tiers. A common European (whole-fund) waterfall: Return of capital : LPs get contributed capital back first. Preferred return : e.g. 8% compounded annually to LPs. GP catch-up : GP receives 100% (or e.g. 50%) until it has carried interest = 20% of profit so far. Residual split : 80/20 (LP/GP). An American (deal-by-deal) waterfall applies the tiers to each investment as it's realised, which pays the GP sooner and usually requires a clawback. Example: LP contributes 100, three years, 8% pref, 20% carry, 100% catch-up, distributions total 200. Tier 1: 100. Tier 2 pref = 100×(1.08³−1) = 25.97. Tier 3 catch-up = 0.2×25.97 ÷ (1−0.2) = 6.49 to GP. Tier 4: remaining 67.54 split 80/20. GP total = 6.49 + 13.51 = 20.00 = 20% of the 100 profit . The waterfall calculator does this for you."},{"k":"module","m":"07","t":"07 · Fees, Expenses & Waterfalls","h":"Return metrics","u":"modules/07-fees-and-waterfalls.html#return-metrics","x":"Metric Formula Reads as MOIC (Distributions + NAV) ÷ paid-in Multiple on invested capital (gross or net) DPI Distributions ÷ paid-in Cash actually returned RVPI NAV ÷ paid-in Unrealised value remaining TVPI DPI + RVPI Total value per unit of paid-in IRR Rate that sets NPV of dated cash flows to zero (XIRR) Time-weighted by cash flow dates; sensitive to timing For hedge funds, performance is normally a time-weighted return (TWR) that strips the impact of investor flows."},{"k":"module","m":"08","t":"08 · Reconciliation & Controls","h":"Reconciliation & Controls","u":"modules/08-reconciliation-controls.html","x":"Reconciliation & Controls Reconciliation is where your books meet reality. If you can't prove the book to an independent source, you don't know your NAV — you're guessing."},{"k":"module","m":"08","t":"08 · Reconciliation & Controls","h":"What gets reconciled","u":"modules/08-reconciliation-controls.html#what-gets-reconciled","x":"Rec Your side Their side Common break causes Position Books by security Broker / custodian statement Unbooked trade, corporate action, settle-date vs trade-date view Cash Cash by account/currency Bank / broker Unmatched settlement, wrong value date, fees not booked Trade Trade blotter Broker confirms / manager Price/quantity/fee differences, allocation splits Margin / collateral Margin posted Broker margin statement Variation margin timing, haircut differences Interest / dividend Accruals Broker income statement Withholding, record-date errors Capital Investor register Transfer agent, bank Missed subs/reds, cut-off Fees Calculated fee Manager recalculation / invoice Basis choice, day count, HWM"},{"k":"module","m":"08","t":"08 · Reconciliation & Controls","h":"The anatomy of a break","u":"modules/08-reconciliation-controls.html#the-anatomy-of-a-break","x":"Identify : security, quantity or amount difference, currency, as-of date. Classify : timing (settlement lag), data (wrong price/FX/identifier), missing event (corporate action or trade), or genuine error on either side. Resolve : book the entry, or ask the counterparty to correct. Keep evidence. Age and escalate : breaks older than policy thresholds (e.g. 5 / 30 / 60 days) go to a manager and appear in the control report. Rule of thumb Fix the cause , not the symptom. If the same break type recurs, change the process (an automated feed, a checklist, an instruction to the broker)."},{"k":"module","m":"08","t":"08 · Reconciliation & Controls","h":"Controls framework","u":"modules/08-reconciliation-controls.html#controls-framework","x":"Segregation of duties : whoever inputs doesn't approve. Four-eyes review on NAV, trades above threshold, manual journal entries, standing data changes. Preventive vs detective : system validation (balanced entries, mandatory fields) vs reconciliations and exception reports. Evidence : every control leaves a dated sign-off and a retained report. Access : role-based permissions; periodic user access reviews; offboarding is immediate. Change management : system configuration changes (fee rules, allocation rules, report logic) tested and approved before production. Business continuity : tested recovery; documented manual fall-back for the NAV."},{"k":"module","m":"08","t":"08 · Reconciliation & Controls","h":"SOC 1 / ISAE 3402","u":"modules/08-reconciliation-controls.html#soc-1-isae-3402","x":"Administrators often undergo a SOC 1 Type II audit (ISAE 3402 abroad): an auditor tests the design and operating effectiveness of control objectives over a period. Clients' auditors rely on it. As a practitioner you'll be asked for evidence of control operation: keep sign-offs complete and timely, because a missed sign-off is a deviation even when the underlying work was right."},{"k":"module","m":"08","t":"08 · Reconciliation & Controls","h":"Common failure patterns","u":"modules/08-reconciliation-controls.html#common-failure-patterns","x":"\"Plugging\" a break with a miscellaneous account, which hides issues. Rec performed on a different date from the NAV (trade-date books vs settle-date broker positions). Relying on one source for both price and reconciliation. Workarounds that live in spreadsheets without review. Staff turnover: process knowledge not documented."},{"k":"module","m":"09","t":"09 · Financial Statements & Audit","h":"Financial Statements & Audit","u":"modules/09-financial-statements.html","x":"Financial Statements & Audit Annual financial statements are the fund's formal record. Funds follow investment company accounting (US GAAP ASC 946 or IFRS with IFRS 10 investment-entity rules)."},{"k":"module","m":"09","t":"09 · Financial Statements & Audit","h":"Statement set","u":"modules/09-financial-statements.html#statement-set","x":"Statement What it shows Statement of Assets and Liabilities (US) / Statement of Financial Position (IFRS) Investments at fair value, cash, receivables, payables, net assets, NAV per share Schedule of Investments Every holding (or those above 5% of NAV with others aggregated) with fair value, % of NAV, country/industry; open derivatives; securities sold short Statement of Operations Investment income, expenses, realised and unrealised gains Statement of Changes in Net Assets Operations plus capital activity (subs/reds, distributions) Statement of Cash Flows May be exempt for investment companies meeting ASC 230 conditions (e.g. substantially all assets are Level 1/2, little debt) Financial Highlights Total return, expense and net investment income ratios, per-share data, by class or investor group Notes Accounting policies, fair value levels, derivatives, related parties, fees, risks, subsequent events"},{"k":"module","m":"09","t":"09 · Financial Statements & Audit","h":"Ratios in financial highlights","u":"modules/09-financial-statements.html#ratios-in-financial-highlights","x":"Total return : time-weighted, computed on a class/investor basis, before/after incentive fee, not annualised for periods under a year. Expense ratio : expenses ÷ average net assets (before and after incentive fee shown separately). Net investment income ratio : net investment income ÷ average net assets. Portfolio turnover : required for mutual funds."},{"k":"module","m":"09","t":"09 · Financial Statements & Audit","h":"Reporting derivatives","u":"modules/09-financial-statements.html#reporting-derivatives","x":"Disclose notional, fair value by contract type and location in the statements, and the effect on operations (ASC 815 / IFRS 7). Offsetting of derivative assets and liabilities needs a legal right of offset (master netting arrangements) and an accounting policy election."},{"k":"module","m":"09","t":"09 · Financial Statements & Audit","h":"The annual audit process","u":"modules/09-financial-statements.html#the-annual-audit-process","x":"Planning : auditor sets scope and materiality, identifies risk areas (valuation of Level 3, completeness of liabilities). PBC list : trial balance, subledger, broker confirmations, bank statements, pricing evidence, investor capital register, fee calculations, board minutes. Fieldwork : auditor independently confirms with brokers/custodians and counterparties; tests valuations; re-performs fee calculations. Draft statements : administrator drafts; manager and board review; auditor issues the opinion. Subsequent events : events after year-end (large redemptions, valuation changes) may need disclosure. Before the audit starts Clear aged breaks, tie capital accounts to NAV, and have each estimate documented. Tidy books make the audit cheaper — and funds care."},{"k":"module","m":"09","t":"09 · Financial Statements & Audit","h":"Tax basics a fund accountant should recognise","u":"modules/09-financial-statements.html#tax-basics-a-fund-accountant-should-recognise","x":"US partnerships: allocate taxable income to partners; K-1s; Section 704(c); wash sale and straddle rules; partnership-level 475(f) elections for traders. Offshore funds: usually tax-transparent for corporate tax but investors may face PFIC/CFC regimes. Withholding on dividends/interest and tax reclaim processes. Book-to-tax differences: unrealised gains, organizational costs, wash sales. Tax is jurisdiction-specific. Treat these as vocabulary, not advice, and defer to your firm's tax team for treatment."},{"k":"module","m":"10","t":"10 · Geneva: The Mental Model","h":"Geneva: The Mental Model","u":"modules/10-geneva-mental-model.html","x":"Geneva: The Mental Model Advent Geneva (now part of SS&C) is a multi-currency, multi-asset portfolio accounting system widely used by hedge fund administrators and managers. You already know the accounting; this module maps it onto how Geneva thinks. Read this first. Geneva is heavily configured per firm: menu names, custom fields, transaction type lists, report layouts and workflows differ between installations and versions. Treat this course as the concepts and vocabulary and verify the exact screens and report names with your Geneva administrator or your firm's own procedure manuals. Each module ends with \"Find it in your Geneva\" prompts to do exactly that."},{"k":"module","m":"10","t":"10 · Geneva: The Mental Model","h":"The big picture","u":"modules/10-geneva-mental-model.html#the-big-picture","x":"Inputs Trades, cash, corporate actions, prices, FX, investor dealing Geneva engine Transactions → lots/positions → ledger (journal entries) Outputs Appraisal, trial balance, P&L, investor allocations, statements Controls Recon vs brokers/bank, period lock, audit trail The pivotal idea is that transactions are the source of truth . Positions, lots, P&L and journal entries are derived from the transaction history plus prices and FX. You don't \"edit a position\"; you fix or add the transactions that produced it."},{"k":"module","m":"10","t":"10 · Geneva: The Mental Model","h":"Core objects","u":"modules/10-geneva-mental-model.html#core-objects","x":"Geneva concept Plain-English meaning Accounting link Portfolio A set of books for one entity (a fund, a master, a feeder, an account). Holds its own positions, cash and ledger. The \"entity\" that has a NAV (Module 03) Portfolio group / consolidation Combines portfolios for reporting (e.g. master + feeders). Master–feeder structure (Module 01) Fund / investor allocation Handles partner or shareholder capital accounts, subs/reds, fee allocation. Often a separate module/set-up. Investor accounting (Module 06) Security master Instrument definitions: identifiers, currency, asset type, coupon, maturity, multiplier, day count. Instruments (Module 04) Transaction An event: buy, sell, short, cover, dividend, interest, expense, journal, transfer, cash in/out, FX etc. Every journal entry begins here Lot (tax lot) A specific purchase with cost, quantity and date. Sales relieve lots using the configured method. FIFO/LIFO/HIFO/average (Module 02) Price & FX tables Daily prices by security and rates by currency, loaded with sources. Pricing (Module 05) Ledger / chart of accounts Accounts that transactions post to via rules, giving trial balance and statements. Double entry (Module 02) Accounting period Month/quarter/year that can be opened and closed (locked). Month-end close (Module 03)"},{"k":"module","m":"10","t":"10 · Geneva: The Mental Model","h":"Two clocks: effective date and knowledge date","u":"modules/10-geneva-mental-model.html#two-clocks-effective-date-and-knowledge-date","x":"Geneva is bitemporal . Every transaction has: Effective date : when it economically happened (trade date, ex-date, etc.). Determines which accounting period it belongs to. Knowledge date : when the system knew about it (when it was entered or changed). This lets you ask two different questions: Question Settings \"What were our positions at 31 March, as best we know now ?\" Period end = 31 Mar; knowledge date = today (latest) \"What did the NAV report show when we released it on 3 April?\" Period end = 31 Mar; knowledge date = 3 Apr (end of day) \"What changed between the estimate and the final?\" Run the same report at two knowledge dates and compare Why seniors love this. It makes back-dated corrections auditable. You can prove what was known when, reproduce a released report exactly, and quantify a NAV error by diffing two knowledge dates. If a report \"doesn't match what we sent\", your first check is the knowledge date."},{"k":"module","m":"10","t":"10 · Geneva: The Mental Model","h":"How a trade flows through Geneva (conceptual)","u":"modules/10-geneva-mental-model.html#how-a-trade-flows-through-geneva-conceptual","x":"Trade transaction recorded with trade date (effective date), settle date, quantity, price, commission, broker, fund allocation. On the trade date Geneva creates/updates a lot and records a payable (settlement liability) per ledger rules. Daily pricing updates market value and unrealized P&L; accrual routines book interest, dividends, financing. On settlement the cash side posts and the payable clears. On sale, lots are relieved by method, realized P&L is computed and unrealized is reversed. Ledger entries aggregate to the trial balance which supports the NAV and financial statements."},{"k":"module","m":"10","t":"10 · Geneva: The Mental Model","h":"Multi-currency and multi-basis","u":"modules/10-geneva-mental-model.html#multi-currency-and-multi-basis","x":"Geneva keeps local-currency and base-currency amounts and applies FX at the effective date. Positions, cost and P&L can be split into price effect and FX effect . Many installations also run more than one accounting basis (e.g. book vs tax) side by side; know which basis a report is run on."},{"k":"module","m":"10","t":"10 · Geneva: The Mental Model","h":"Audit trail and permissions","u":"modules/10-geneva-mental-model.html#audit-trail-and-permissions","x":"Changes to transactions, prices, rules and users are logged. Role-based access separates people who enter, approve and close. Use the audit trail to answer \"who changed this and when?\" before making any correction."},{"k":"module","m":"10","t":"10 · Geneva: The Mental Model","h":"Find it in your Geneva","u":"modules/10-geneva-mental-model.html#find-it-in-your-geneva","x":"Which portfolios and portfolio groups exist for the funds you work on? Draw them. Open one trade and find: effective date, settle date, lot, ledger entries generated. Run the same report with today's knowledge date and with last month-end's release date. What differs and why? Find where lot-relief method is configured, and what it is for your funds. Locate the chart of accounts and match three accounts to the categories in Module 02."},{"k":"module","m":"11","t":"11 · Geneva: Daily & Month-End Workflow","h":"Geneva: Daily & Month-End Workflow","u":"modules/11-geneva-daily-workflow.html","x":"Geneva: Daily & Month-End Workflow A repeatable routine is what makes a senior fund accountant fast and safe. This is a template you adapt to your firm's procedures. Verify names locally. The sequence below is the standard logic. Exact menus, import tools and report names depend on your firm's Geneva set-up."},{"k":"module","m":"11","t":"11 · Geneva: Daily & Month-End Workflow","h":"Daily checklist","u":"modules/11-geneva-daily-workflow.html#daily-checklist","x":"# Step What \"good\" looks like Typical failure 1 Load trades from manager/OMS/broker files; review rejects and unmatched items. Every trade on the manager blotter appears once, correct fund, correct side/qty/price. Duplicate import; wrong identifier; trade booked to the wrong portfolio. 2 Load prices and FX ; run missing/stale/large-move checks. No holdings at zero or missing; all exceptions explained. Pence vs pounds; stale price; wrong FX cut. 3 Process corporate actions and income (dividends, coupons, maturities, paydowns). Calendar items processed on ex-date with correct rate and withholding. Missed split → huge P&L swing. 4 Book cash movements and expenses; clear pending items. Cash by currency ties to bank/broker statements. Unbooked fees; wrong value date. 5 Run accruals (interest, financing, fees). Accrual = balance × rate × days basis within rounding. Rate reset missed; day count wrong. 6 Reconcile positions & cash to prime broker/custodian. All breaks explained and ageing within policy. Trade-date vs settle-date comparison mismatch. 7 Review the draft NAV (appraisal, P&L attribution, exposure). Moves explained; reviewer signs off. Self-review; checks skipped on busy days. 8 Release & archive reports with the knowledge date used. Reproducible later. Reports saved without the parameters."},{"k":"module","m":"11","t":"11 · Geneva: Daily & Month-End Workflow","h":"Month-end additions","u":"modules/11-geneva-daily-workflow.html#month-end-additions","x":"Final pricing and FX at the month-end cut; resolve all challenges. Investor activity : record subscriptions and redemptions; confirm with the transfer agent / bank. Fees : run management and performance fee calculations; reconcile to your independent calc (Module 07). Allocation : run the P&L allocation to investors; confirm capital accounts sum to NAV. True-ups : expenses, amortisation, accrual reversals. Review package : trial balance, appraisal, P&L, capital rollforward, rec status, aged breaks. Close the period : lock it once approved so no late changes sneak in. Deliver NAV, statements, support schedules."},{"k":"module","m":"11","t":"11 · Geneva: Daily & Month-End Workflow","h":"Corrections after the fact","u":"modules/11-geneva-daily-workflow.html#corrections-after-the-fact","x":"With a bitemporal system you can enter a back-dated transaction without overwriting history. The safe routine: Find the root cause and confirm in the audit trail how the data got there. Check whether the period is closed; reopen only with approval and document it. Enter the correction with the right effective date ; Geneva records today as the knowledge date . Re-run affected reports at the old and new knowledge dates to quantify impact by day; take it to the NAV-error process (Module 03). Re-run allocations and fees for any period affected. Order matters. Fix upstream data first (trades, prices, corporate actions), then recalc accruals, fees and allocations. Fixing allocations before the portfolio-level error is resolved just moves the problem around."},{"k":"module","m":"11","t":"11 · Geneva: Daily & Month-End Workflow","h":"Speed habits for seniors","u":"modules/11-geneva-daily-workflow.html#speed-habits-for-seniors","x":"Keep saved report templates with parameters set (period, knowledge date, basis, currency). Build a standing exception list : items that always need attention (illiquid prices, FX forwards, swap resets). Keep a day-by-day change log for each fund: \"what was different this month\". Write the \"why\" in the transaction comment/field where available; your future self and auditor will thank you."},{"k":"module","m":"11","t":"11 · Geneva: Daily & Month-End Workflow","h":"Find it in your Geneva","u":"modules/11-geneva-daily-workflow.html#find-it-in-your-geneva","x":"Where do trade files land, and which job/process loads them? What does a rejected record look like? Where are prices loaded; what are the primary/backup sources; what is the exception report called? How is the period locked, who can reopen it, and how is that logged? Write your own one-page \"daily checklist\" with real screen and report names."},{"k":"module","m":"12","t":"12 · Geneva: Reports, Recs & Troubleshooting","h":"Geneva: Reports, Recs & Troubleshooting","u":"modules/12-geneva-reports-recs.html","x":"Geneva: Reports, Recs & Troubleshooting Geneva is only as useful as your ability to ask it the right question. This module is a reference: which report answers which accounting question, and how to diagnose a break. Report names vary. The categories below are the standard ones in portfolio accounting systems and exist in Geneva in some form (standard, Report Writer-based, or custom). Look up the actual names in your own report library."},{"k":"module","m":"12","t":"12 · Geneva: Reports, Recs & Troubleshooting","h":"Question → report type","u":"modules/12-geneva-reports-recs.html#question-report-type","x":"You want to know… Report type Check What do we hold and what is it worth? Portfolio appraisal / holdings report Quantity vs broker, price vs source, FX, % of NAV Does the ledger balance and what's the NAV? Trial balance, statement of net assets Debits = credits; NAV ties to investor capital What drove performance? Realized/unrealized gain-loss, P&L by position/strategy Largest contributors; price vs FX effect What happened to cash? Cash activity / cash balances by currency Ties to bank and broker statements How did the position move between two dates? Position / lot rollforward (opening + buys − sells ± CA = closing) Each movement traces to a transaction What are the journal entries behind a number? Journal / ledger detail Account mapping correct What did we accrue? Accrual / income report, expense accruals Rate × balance × days Who owns what? Investor capital rollforward / allocation report Sum = NAV; fees right What changed since last release? Same report at two knowledge dates; transaction audit report Every change has a reason"},{"k":"module","m":"12","t":"12 · Geneva: Reports, Recs & Troubleshooting","h":"Report parameters that bite","u":"modules/12-geneva-reports-recs.html#report-parameters-that-bite","x":"Period start/end (effective date) and knowledge date . Portfolio vs group : is the report consolidating? Basis (e.g. book vs tax) and currency (local vs base). Trade-date vs settle-date view : critical for broker reconciliations. Include/exclude pending or unsettled items, closed positions, zero balances."},{"k":"module","m":"12","t":"12 · Geneva: Reports, Recs & Troubleshooting","h":"Reconciling Geneva to the outside world","u":"modules/12-geneva-reports-recs.html#reconciling-geneva-to-the-outside-world","x":"Positions Run the Geneva holdings on the same date and basis (settle-date for custodian comparison; trade-date for broker trade positions). Match by identifier (CUSIP/ISIN/SEDOL/FIGI) and account; mismatched identifiers create false breaks. Explain remaining differences: unbooked trade, unprocessed corporate action, free-of-payment transfers, short vs long sign. Cash Compare by currency and account on value date. Look for: missing fees and interest, FX settlement legs, margin movements, dividends and withholding differences. Ledger vs administrator or manager books Compare trial balances. Differences usually trace to classification (realized vs unrealized), accrual timing, or different treatment of fees."},{"k":"module","m":"12","t":"12 · Geneva: Reports, Recs & Troubleshooting","h":"Troubleshooting playbook","u":"modules/12-geneva-reports-recs.html#troubleshooting-playbook","x":"Symptom First checks NAV moved far more than the market P&L by position, top 10 movers: stale or wrong prices; missing corporate action (splits!); wrong FX; duplicate trade Position quantity differs from broker Trade vs settle-date view; unbooked trade/CA; transfers; short/long sign; identifier mismatch Realized P&L looks wrong on a sale Lot relief method; which lots were relieved; cost basis after corporate actions; FX in cost Cash negative Unbooked inflow (subscription, sale settlement), FX trade leg, wrong value date Accrued interest differs from broker Day-count convention; coupon schedule; settlement vs trade date; purchased-accrued treatment Capital accounts don't sum to NAV Allocation run on older data; late transaction after allocation; fees not allocated; rounding on many investors Report differs from what was sent Knowledge date used; period; basis; was the period reopened? Import fails or loads fewer rows File format change; unknown identifier; missing required field; duplicate detection Journal entry posted to unexpected account Transaction type/rule mapping; security master asset class; custom field Method. Bisect. Is the difference in quantity, price, FX, or accrual? Which date does it first appear? Which transaction's knowledge date is after your last release? Narrowing the where-and-when almost always names the cause."},{"k":"module","m":"12","t":"12 · Geneva: Reports, Recs & Troubleshooting","h":"Report Writer and custom reporting","u":"modules/12-geneva-reports-recs.html#report-writer-and-custom-reporting","x":"Geneva includes a report-writing capability to build or modify layouts, plus data export for analysis in Excel/BI. Senior habits: Never overwrite a production report; copy, change, test against a known period, then promote under change control. Document the logic of every custom column (what's the source, what's excluded). Build a few validation reports whose only job is to catch breaks (e.g., positions with no price, flows not allocated, unbalanced investor capital)."},{"k":"module","m":"12","t":"12 · Geneva: Reports, Recs & Troubleshooting","h":"Find it in your Geneva","u":"modules/12-geneva-reports-recs.html#find-it-in-your-geneva","x":"List the 10 reports you use most, and map each to the question table above. Find the report for a lot-level rollforward and trace one sale from purchase lot to realized P&L. Reproduce last month-end's NAV report exactly. Which parameters did you need? Build the first draft of your own \"break triage\" decision tree based on the playbook table."},{"k":"module","m":"13","t":"13 · Thinking Like a Senior","h":"Thinking Like a Senior","u":"modules/13-thinking-like-a-senior.html","x":"Thinking Like a Senior Seniority is less about knowing more instruments and more about judgement: what to check, what to question, when to escalate, and how to leave the process better than you found it."},{"k":"module","m":"13","t":"13 · Thinking Like a Senior","h":"From preparer to reviewer","u":"modules/13-thinking-like-a-senior.html#from-preparer-to-reviewer","x":"A preparer asks \"did I enter it correctly?\" A reviewer asks \" would I notice if this were wrong? \" Reviewing is a skill with a method: Start from expectation : before opening the numbers, estimate the answer (NAV change from benchmark moves, fee from NAV × rate). Look at movement, not just balances : day-on-day and month-on-month; what changed and why. Prioritise by risk : size × complexity × novelty. New instruments, new counterparties, manual overrides, and Level 3 deserve more time. Test the controls, not just the output : was the sign-off done, were the exceptions cleared? Leave evidence : a note saying what you checked is the product of the review."},{"k":"module","m":"13","t":"13 · Thinking Like a Senior","h":"Materiality and judgement","u":"modules/13-thinking-like-a-senior.html#materiality-and-judgement","x":"Materiality has quantitative (percent of NAV or of an investor's balance) and qualitative aspects (a small error that hides a breach, a related-party issue, or a trend). Thresholds come from policy and the manager/board; you apply and escalate, you don't invent them. When in doubt, escalate early with facts: what, size, cause, proposed fix, deadline."},{"k":"module","m":"13","t":"13 · Thinking Like a Senior","h":"Escalation template","u":"modules/13-thinking-like-a-senior.html#escalation-template","x":"What: NAV for Fund X on 12-Mar is overstated by $182k (0.31% of NAV) Cause: Corporate action (2-for-1 split in ABC) not processed on ex-date Impact: Dealt on 12-Mar: 2 subscriptions ($1.1m), 1 redemption ($0.4m) Proposed: Back-date CA, re-strike NAV, compensate per policy; fund materiality threshold 0.5% Need from you: approval to proceed; manager confirmation of position Deadline: Today 15:00 (NAV distribution cut-off)"},{"k":"module","m":"13","t":"13 · Thinking Like a Senior","h":"Communication with managers, investors and auditors","u":"modules/13-thinking-like-a-senior.html#communication-with-managers-investors-and-auditors","x":"Be specific and neutral : \"Position differs by 5,000 shares vs PB; likely unbooked trade of 3 Mar; please confirm\" beats \"your trade is missing\". Own the timeline : \"I will update by 14:00.\" Separate fact from view : valuation views are the manager's, evidence is yours. Write for the auditor : any adjustment should be explainable without you in the room."},{"k":"module","m":"13","t":"13 · Thinking Like a Senior","h":"Ownership of the close","u":"modules/13-thinking-like-a-senior.html#ownership-of-the-close","x":"Run the close from a calendar with named owners and cut-offs, working backwards from the NAV release. Track open items on a visible list with age and owner. Hold a short retrospective on every miss: cause, fix, prevention, owner."},{"k":"module","m":"13","t":"13 · Thinking Like a Senior","h":"Improving the process","u":"modules/13-thinking-like-a-senior.html#improving-the-process","x":"Automate repetitive checks (price tolerance reports, rec auto-match, capital tie-out). Standardise templates and checklists across funds. Document tribal knowledge : one-page procedures for the 20 things only one person knows. Measure : NAV on time %, errors per 100 NAVs, aged breaks, rework hours."},{"k":"module","m":"13","t":"13 · Thinking Like a Senior","h":"Developing your career","u":"modules/13-thinking-like-a-senior.html#developing-your-career","x":"Level Focus Associate Accurate processing, learning systems, clearing breaks. Senior Reviewing, complex instruments, investor-level calcs, training others. Manager People, client relationship, process design, risk. Credentials that help: CPA/ACCA, CFA (levels I–II give a valuable investment foundation), and CAIA for alternatives. Pair them with deep system skills (Geneva, plus SQL/Excel/Python for analysis). A 90-day plan Month 1: master your funds' daily checklists and reconcile everything you touch. Month 2: lead one month-end including fees and capital allocation. Month 3: find one recurring break, fix its root cause, and document the new process."},{"k":"term","m":"02","t":"Glossary","h":"Accrual","u":"glossary.html?q=Accrual","x":"Recognising income/expense when earned or incurred rather than when cash moves."},{"k":"term","m":"04","t":"Glossary","h":"Accrued interest","u":"glossary.html?q=Accrued%20interest","x":"Interest earned on a bond since the last coupon date but not yet paid."},{"k":"term","m":"01","t":"Glossary","h":"Administrator","u":"glossary.html?q=Administrator","x":"Independent provider that keeps the fund's books, calculates NAV and processes investor dealing."},{"k":"term","m":"06","t":"Glossary","h":"Allocation (P&L)","u":"glossary.html?q=Allocation%20(P%26L)","x":"Distribution of fund P&L, fees and expenses to investors' capital accounts or classes."},{"k":"term","m":"12","t":"Glossary","h":"Appraisal","u":"glossary.html?q=Appraisal","x":"Holdings report showing positions, prices and market values (common portfolio-system report)."},{"k":"term","m":"05","t":"Glossary","h":"ASC 820","u":"glossary.html?q=ASC%20820","x":"US GAAP fair value measurement standard (Level 1/2/3 hierarchy)."},{"k":"term","m":"09","t":"Glossary","h":"ASC 946","u":"glossary.html?q=ASC%20946","x":"US GAAP guidance for investment companies."},{"k":"term","m":"10","t":"Glossary","h":"Basis (accounting)","u":"glossary.html?q=Basis%20(accounting)","x":"A set of accounting rules applied to the same transactions, e.g. book vs tax."},{"k":"term","m":"05","t":"Glossary","h":"Bid / ask","u":"glossary.html?q=Bid%20%2F%20ask","x":"Prices a buyer will pay / a seller will accept. Longs often valued at bid, shorts at ask, per policy."},{"k":"term","m":"08","t":"Glossary","h":"Break","u":"glossary.html?q=Break","x":"A difference between two sources that should agree (e.g. book vs broker)."},{"k":"term","m":"07","t":"Glossary","h":"Carried interest","u":"glossary.html?q=Carried%20interest","x":"The GP's share of profits in a private fund, typically 20%."},{"k":"term","m":"07","t":"Glossary","h":"Catch-up","u":"glossary.html?q=Catch-up","x":"Waterfall tier where the GP receives a high share until it has its full carry share of profits to date."},{"k":"term","m":"04","t":"Glossary","h":"Clean / dirty price","u":"glossary.html?q=Clean%20%2F%20dirty%20price","x":"Bond price excluding / including accrued interest."},{"k":"term","m":"07","t":"Glossary","h":"Clawback","u":"glossary.html?q=Clawback","x":"Obligation to return previously received incentive/carry if later results warrant."},{"k":"term","m":"04","t":"Glossary","h":"Corporate action","u":"glossary.html?q=Corporate%20action","x":"Issuer event such as a split, merger, spin-off or dividend that changes positions or cost."},{"k":"term","m":"07","t":"Glossary","h":"Crystallization","u":"glossary.html?q=Crystallization","x":"Date accrued performance fee becomes payable."},{"k":"term","m":"01","t":"Glossary","h":"Custodian","u":"glossary.html?q=Custodian","x":"Bank that safekeeps a fund's assets and settles trades."},{"k":"term","m":"04","t":"Glossary","h":"Day count","u":"glossary.html?q=Day%20count","x":"Convention to compute interest days and year length (30/360, ACT/360, ACT/365, ACT/ACT)."},{"k":"term","m":"07","t":"Glossary","h":"DPI","u":"glossary.html?q=DPI","x":"Distributions to paid-in capital."},{"k":"term","m":"10","t":"Glossary","h":"Effective date","u":"glossary.html?q=Effective%20date","x":"In Geneva, the date a transaction economically occurred; places it in a period."},{"k":"term","m":"06","t":"Glossary","h":"Equalization","u":"glossary.html?q=Equalization","x":"Mechanism letting investors in a single NAV class pay performance fees that match their own gains."},{"k":"term","m":"02","t":"Glossary","h":"Ex-date","u":"glossary.html?q=Ex-date","x":"First date a stock trades without the right to the declared dividend."},{"k":"term","m":"05","t":"Glossary","h":"Fair value","u":"glossary.html?q=Fair%20value","x":"Price received to sell an asset in an orderly transaction between market participants."},{"k":"term","m":"01","t":"Glossary","h":"Feeder fund","u":"glossary.html?q=Feeder%20fund","x":"Fund that invests substantially all assets in a master fund."},{"k":"term","m":"02","t":"Glossary","h":"FIFO / LIFO / HIFO","u":"glossary.html?q=FIFO%20%2F%20LIFO%20%2F%20HIFO","x":"Lot relief methods: first-in, last-in, highest-in first-out."},{"k":"term","m":"06","t":"Glossary","h":"Gate","u":"glossary.html?q=Gate","x":"Limit on total redemptions in a dealing period."},{"k":"term","m":"03","t":"Glossary","h":"GAV","u":"glossary.html?q=GAV","x":"Gross asset value; assets before certain deductions."},{"k":"term","m":"10","t":"Glossary","h":"Geneva","u":"glossary.html?q=Geneva","x":"Advent Geneva (SS&C): portfolio accounting system widely used by hedge fund managers and administrators."},{"k":"term","m":"01","t":"Glossary","h":"GP","u":"glossary.html?q=GP","x":"General partner; controls the partnership and appoints service providers."},{"k":"term","m":"07","t":"Glossary","h":"High-water mark (HWM)","u":"glossary.html?q=High-water%20mark%20(HWM)","x":"Highest prior NAV level that must be exceeded before a performance fee is charged."},{"k":"term","m":"07","t":"Glossary","h":"Hurdle rate","u":"glossary.html?q=Hurdle%20rate","x":"Minimum return before a performance fee applies (hard or soft)."},{"k":"term","m":"07","t":"Glossary","h":"IRR","u":"glossary.html?q=IRR","x":"Internal rate of return; discount rate that sets NPV of dated cash flows to zero."},{"k":"term","m":"10","t":"Glossary","h":"Knowledge date","u":"glossary.html?q=Knowledge%20date","x":"In Geneva, the date the system learned of a transaction; supports as-known-at reporting."},{"k":"term","m":"05","t":"Glossary","h":"Level 1/2/3","u":"glossary.html?q=Level%201%2F2%2F3","x":"Fair value hierarchy tiers by observability of inputs."},{"k":"term","m":"06","t":"Glossary","h":"Lock-up","u":"glossary.html?q=Lock-up","x":"Period when investors cannot redeem."},{"k":"term","m":"02","t":"Glossary","h":"Lot","u":"glossary.html?q=Lot","x":"A specific purchase with its own cost and date used to compute realized P&L."},{"k":"term","m":"07","t":"Glossary","h":"Management fee","u":"glossary.html?q=Management%20fee","x":"Fee on NAV (or commitments) for managing the fund."},{"k":"term","m":"01","t":"Glossary","h":"Master fund","u":"glossary.html?q=Master%20fund","x":"Fund holding the portfolio for one or more feeders."},{"k":"term","m":"07","t":"Glossary","h":"MOIC","u":"glossary.html?q=MOIC","x":"Multiple on invested capital."},{"k":"term","m":"03","t":"Glossary","h":"NAV","u":"glossary.html?q=NAV","x":"Net asset value = assets − liabilities."},{"k":"term","m":"03","t":"Glossary","h":"NAV error","u":"glossary.html?q=NAV%20error","x":"A NAV that was wrong when released; assessed against materiality and corrected per policy."},{"k":"term","m":"04","t":"Glossary","h":"OID","u":"glossary.html?q=OID","x":"Original issue discount; accreted to income over the bond's life."},{"k":"term","m":"09","t":"Glossary","h":"PBC","u":"glossary.html?q=PBC","x":"Provided-by-client list for the auditor."},{"k":"term","m":"07","t":"Glossary","h":"Performance fee","u":"glossary.html?q=Performance%20fee","x":"Incentive fee on profits (also incentive fee or carry in PE)."},{"k":"term","m":"07","t":"Glossary","h":"Pref (preferred return)","u":"glossary.html?q=Pref%20(preferred%20return)","x":"Minimum return LPs receive before the GP shares in profits."},{"k":"term","m":"01","t":"Glossary","h":"Prime broker","u":"glossary.html?q=Prime%20broker","x":"Broker providing custody, financing, stock borrow and clearing to hedge funds."},{"k":"term","m":"02","t":"Glossary","h":"Realized / unrealized","u":"glossary.html?q=Realized%20%2F%20unrealized","x":"Gain locked in by a sale vs change in value of positions still held."},{"k":"term","m":"06","t":"Glossary","h":"Redemption","u":"glossary.html?q=Redemption","x":"Investor withdrawal of capital at a dealing-day NAV."},{"k":"term","m":"12","t":"Glossary","h":"Report Writer","u":"glossary.html?q=Report%20Writer","x":"Geneva's capability to build and modify reports."},{"k":"term","m":"07","t":"Glossary","h":"RVPI","u":"glossary.html?q=RVPI","x":"Residual value (NAV) to paid-in capital."},{"k":"term","m":"06","t":"Glossary","h":"Series accounting","u":"glossary.html?q=Series%20accounting","x":"Issuing each subscription month as a separate series to track its own fee base."},{"k":"term","m":"02","t":"Glossary","h":"Settlement date","u":"glossary.html?q=Settlement%20date","x":"Date cash and securities change hands."},{"k":"term","m":"06","t":"Glossary","h":"Side pocket","u":"glossary.html?q=Side%20pocket","x":"Segregated class for illiquid investments."},{"k":"term","m":"08","t":"Glossary","h":"SOC 1","u":"glossary.html?q=SOC%201","x":"Audit report on controls relevant to clients' financial reporting."},{"k":"term","m":"05","t":"Glossary","h":"Stale price","u":"glossary.html?q=Stale%20price","x":"A price unchanged for longer than expected, suggesting it isn't current."},{"k":"term","m":"06","t":"Glossary","h":"Subscription","u":"glossary.html?q=Subscription","x":"Investor purchase of fund interests at a dealing-day NAV."},{"k":"term","m":"04","t":"Glossary","h":"Total-return swap","u":"glossary.html?q=Total-return%20swap","x":"Swap exchanging the return on a reference asset for a financing rate."},{"k":"term","m":"02","t":"Glossary","h":"Trade date","u":"glossary.html?q=Trade%20date","x":"Date the fund is committed to a trade; basis for booking."},{"k":"term","m":"01","t":"Glossary","h":"Transfer agent","u":"glossary.html?q=Transfer%20agent","x":"Maintains the shareholder register."},{"k":"term","m":"07","t":"Glossary","h":"TVPI","u":"glossary.html?q=TVPI","x":"Total value (distributions + NAV) to paid-in."},{"k":"term","m":"04","t":"Glossary","h":"Variation margin","u":"glossary.html?q=Variation%20margin","x":"Daily cash settlement of futures/cleared derivative P&L."},{"k":"term","m":"07","t":"Glossary","h":"Waterfall","u":"glossary.html?q=Waterfall","x":"Ordered distribution of proceeds among LPs and GP."},{"k":"tool","m":"","t":"Calculators","h":"NAV and NAV per share calculator","u":"tools.html#nav","x":"net asset value assets minus liabilities shares"},{"k":"tool","m":"","t":"Calculators","h":"Bond accrued interest calculator","u":"tools.html#accrual","x":"accrued interest coupon day count 30/360 ACT/360 ACT/365 ACT/ACT clean dirty price"},{"k":"tool","m":"","t":"Calculators","h":"Management and performance fee calculator","u":"tools.html#perf","x":"management fee performance fee incentive high-water mark HWM hurdle soft hard crystallization"},{"k":"tool","m":"","t":"Calculators","h":"Private-fund waterfall calculator","u":"tools.html#wf","x":"waterfall preferred return pref catch-up carried interest carry GP LP distribution"},{"k":"tool","m":"","t":"Calculators","h":"IRR, MOIC, DPI, TVPI calculator","u":"tools.html#ret","x":"IRR XIRR MOIC DPI RVPI TVPI returns private equity"},{"k":"tool","m":"","t":"Calculators","h":"P&L allocation calculator","u":"tools.html#alloc","x":"allocate profit loss investors capital pro rata weight"},{"k":"tool","m":"","t":"Calculators","h":"Journal entry trainer","u":"tools.html#je","x":"journal entry debit credit practice buy sell accrual dividend redemption unrealized"}];
